  "body": {
    "id": "prop:unavoidable-harm-iff",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal D_{L,\\kappa}\\) with arm-cell vector \\(\\mu\\), \\(H_-(\\mu)>0\\) if and only if there exists \\(d\\in\\{1,\\ldots,L\\}\\) such that \\(q_{0d}-q_{1d}>R_d-C_d\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "L",
      "kappa",
      "P",
      "Dclass",
      "mu",
      "Hlo",
      "q0",
      "q1",
      "R",
      "C"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:observable-primitives",
      "def:observable-domain",
      "thm:closed-form-harm-interval"
    ],
    "justification": "The iff inequality turns a latent cross-world conclusion into an observable sorting-capacity certificate.",
    "gap": "Published probability-of-causation bounds and harm bounds across observed outcome categories, including de Aguas--Krumscheid--Pensar--Biele, do not provide a necessary-and-sufficient sorting-capacity condition for harm within latent stayers at protocol-assigned selection tiers.",
    "consumer": "An applied researcher can diagnose unavoidable harm layer by layer without solving a latent allocation problem.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal D_{L,\\kappa}\\).  We use algebraic reduction of the already-established sharp lower endpoint.  Compatibility and the diagonal-box theorem give \\(\\ell_d\\le u_d\\), so\n\\[\n \\min\\{u_d,\\max\\{\\ell_d,a_d\\}\\}\\ge\\ell_d.\n\\]\nThe positive-stayer-floor condition therefore implies\n\\[\n \\sum_{d=1}^{L}\\min\\{u_d,\\max\\{\\ell_d,a_d\\}\\}\n \\ge\\sum_{d=1}^{L}\\ell_d\n \\ge\\kappa>0.\n\\]\nDivision in the closed-form lower endpoint is consequently valid.  The closed-form harm-interval theorem gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n H_-(\\mu)>0\n &\\Longleftrightarrow\n \\sum_{d=1}^{L}(\\ell_d-a_d)_+>0\\\\\n &\\Longleftrightarrow\n \\ell_d>a_d\\quad\\text{for at least one }d.\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le q_{0d}\\le r_d\\) and \\(q_{1d}\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n a_d=r_d-q_{0d}+q_{1d}\\ge0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\ell_d>a_d\\) forces, and is forced by, the positive branch of \\(\\ell_d\\) exceeding \\(a_d\\):\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\ell_d>a_d\n &\\Longleftrightarrow C_d-R_{d-1}>r_d-q_{0d}+q_{1d}\\\\\n &\\Longleftrightarrow q_{0d}-q_{1d}>R_{d-1}+r_d-C_d\\\\\n &\\Longleftrightarrow q_{0d}-q_{1d}>R_d-C_d.\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTaking the existential quantifier over \\(d=1,\\ldots,L\\) proves both directions of the certificate.",
